From 185d956bc382f9d3a1b41b47b8cf3508e3fe519d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 02:41:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 35/36] Give the stream window a Wayland connection of its own

The stream surface was created on Qt's Wayland display. Qt 6 dispatches that
display on an event thread of its own, and libwayland allows one dispatcher per
queue, so the video path and Qt raced on every event: the VeriSilicon WSI saw a
second release fence for a buffer it had not finished with, which is the
buffer_fenced_release assertion that ended a game, and a frame descriptor that
the flush path closed after a full socket turned into "the Wayland connection
experienced a fatal error: Bad file descriptor" for the whole application.

The surface now connects for itself. That gives it one owner for its events, so
the buffer cache is only touched from the interface thread, and it makes the
descriptor lifetime trivial: a frame descriptor stays open until the flush that
carries it succeeds. The socket is drained without blocking from the frame
clock, so buffer releases arrive at 60 Hz instead of whenever Qt happens to
dispatch, and a compositor that really goes away now ends the stream with a
message instead of a frozen window.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]
